-- Q5001: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because the boot relaxation rule permits up to 20% non-stock consideration when 80% is voting stock","Yes, because any combination of stock and cash is permitted in a Type C reorganization","No, because a Type C reorganization requires 100% voting stock with no boot exceptions","No, because the $30 million cash component exceeds the de minimis threshold for boot"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 368(a)(2)(B), a Type C reorganization permits non-stock consideration (boot) if at least 80% of the target''s gross assets are acquired solely for voting stock. However, any assumed liabilities count as boot for the 80% test, which can cause failure if total non-stock consideration exceeds 20%.
Wrong (B): Type C reorganizations have a strict ''solely for voting stock'' requirement with only the limited boot relaxation exception, not unlimited flexibility.
Wrong (C): The 100% requirement is the general rule, but Section 368(a)(2)(B) provides a specific boot relaxation exception permitting up to 20%.
Wrong (D): There is no $10 million de minimis threshold in the Type C reorganization rules; the test is based on the 80% percentage of gross asset value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5001;

-- Q5002: Entity Formation and Restructuring [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Using a distribution to circumvent the continuity of interest requirement","Using a spin-off principally as a device for distributing E&P as capital gains instead of dividends","Transferring assets between related corporations without recognizing gain","Acquiring a loss corporation solely to use its net operating losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 355(a)(1)(B), the device test prevents taxpayers from using corporate divisions (spin-offs, split-offs, split-ups) primarily as a mechanism to distribute earnings and profits at capital gains rates rather than as ordinary dividends. The IRS examines objective factors such as subsequent sales of distributed stock.
Wrong (A): The continuity of interest requirement is a separate doctrine under Section 368, not the purpose of the device test in Section 355.
Wrong (C): Section 351 and related provisions address nonrecognition transfers between related corporations, which is distinct from the device limitation.
Wrong (D): Section 269 addresses acquisitions made to obtain tax benefits like NOLs, not the device test under Section 355.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5002;

-- Q5003: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A Type B stock-for-stock reorganization, preserving the target''s historical asset basis","A Type A statutory merger with no boot, carrying over the target''s existing basis","A taxable asset purchase directly from Target, triggering corporate and shareholder-level tax","A taxable stock purchase with a Section 338(h)(10) election for an eligible target entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 338(h)(10), a buyer who acquires target stock can elect to treat the purchase as a deemed asset sale, obtaining a stepped-up basis in assets. This election avoids double taxation that occurs with a direct asset purchase because the stock sale is disregarded and only the deemed asset sale is recognized on the target''s final return.
Wrong (A): A Type B reorganization is tax-free and preserves the target''s carryover basis in assets, providing no step-up.
Wrong (B): A Type A merger is also tax-free, meaning the acquiring corporation takes a carryover basis rather than a stepped-up basis.
Wrong (C): A direct taxable asset purchase provides a step-up but triggers two levels of tax: corporate-level gain on the sale plus shareholder-level gain on liquidation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5003;

-- Q5953: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
